# <%= current_page.data.title %>

## What WCAG says:

> If keyboard focus can be moved to a component of the page using a keyboard interface, then focus can be moved away from that component using only a keyboard interface, and, if it requires more than unmodified arrow or tab keys or other standard exit methods, the user is advised of the method for moving focus away.

Check the official guidance for [Understanding 2.1.2 No Keyboard Trap](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG22/Understanding/no-keyboard-trap)

## What this means

Some users do not use a mouse or touchscreen, and navigate websites using keyboard commands such as the Tab, Shift+Tab, and arrow keys.

Users who only use keyboards must be able to navigate in and out of page components and not get 'trapped' where they would need to use a mouse or touchscreen – they must always have a way to move on.

## Why it matters

This matters because some people with vision impairments or physical disabilities rely on a keyboard to use the web and cannot use a mouse (or other devices like a touch screen).

## How to check

You can check for a keyboard trap by using the Tab key to move through the page.

When you reach an interactive component (such as a date picker or video player), move into it. Next try and leave it by only using the keyboard (Tab, Shift + Tab, arrow keys and Esc) and continue to the next part of the page.

[How to test in detail for 2.1.2 No Keyboard Trap](https://github.com/alphagov/guide-to-wcag/wiki/2.1.2)

## Common mistake

### A date picker that cannot be dismissed using a keyboard

A user tabs to a date picker in a form and presses Tab to move into the widget. They use the arrow keys to choose a date and press Tab again, but nothing happens. They try every key, but they are stuck inside the date picker. The only way to continue is to click or tap somewhere else.

This widget would fail because the user is stuck inside the date picker if they are only using a keyboard. This has stopped them from completing their user journey.

